# coord-web-dist-build — fires coord-web-dist-build.service (Type=oneshot) on
# a cadence. See that unit's header, and deploy/coord-web-dist-build.sh's
# header, for the full rationale.
#
# 10 minutes (was 1 minute — #2122). The #1543 acceptance target ("live...
# within a minute") picked its cadence from a *desire*, not from how often
# there was ever anything to do: at 1min this fired 53 times/hour on
# dellserver, every single one logging "up to date ... nothing to build",
# which is what buried the host journal (#2122's own investigation was
# slowed by exactly this noise). This unit does not exist to react to every
# commit on `main` — only to ones that touch coord/dashboard/webapp/, and
# #2122 measured that directly against this repo's own history:
#
#   - ALL merges to main: median gap ~41min, but bursts land seconds apart
#     (some measured 7s apart) — polling that tightly buys nothing, since a
#     burst still resolves to one `origin/main` SHA by the time any one
#     tick runs.
#   - Merges that touch coord/dashboard/webapp/ specifically — the only
#     ones this unit's build is *for* — over the trailing 30 days: ~1/day,
#     median gap ~14.3h, and the SHORTEST gap between two such merges in
#     that entire window was ~39min.
#
# 10 minutes stays well under that observed floor (39min), so this cadence
# is never the reason a real webapp change sits unbuilt for anywhere close
# to as long as merges to webapp/ actually take to arrive — while cutting
# ticks from ~1,270/day to ~144/day (~8.8x). Still safe at a tight-ish
# cadence for two reasons a much slower poll (like coord-drive-queue.timer's
# 3min) doesn't need to justify:
#
#   1. Most fires are a no-op: the script compares origin/main's resolved SHA
#      against the currently-published release and exits immediately if
#      nothing merged since the last run — no npm invocation, no I/O beyond
#      one `git fetch` + `rev-parse`, and (#2122) no journal line either; it
#      only updates a heartbeat file (see coord-web-dist-build.sh's
#      HEARTBEAT_FILE) so a dead trigger stays distinguishable from "quiet
#      because nothing merged" — see
#      coord.health.checks.deploy_lane_facts.probe_webapp_build_heartbeat.
#   2. The script's own flock means a slow build (network hiccup during
#      `npm ci`, etc.) can't stack with the next fire — a busy run is
#      detected and the new invocation exits 0 immediately, exactly like
#      coord-drive-queue's tick.
#
# A build that actually runs (something under coord/dashboard/webapp/**
# merged) takes on the order of tens of seconds; that run and the next may
# overlap the flock, in which case the next fire simply retries 10 minutes
# later.
#
# Install: see deploy/coord-web-dist-build.sh's header, or
# docs/PHONE_WEBAPP.md / docs/AGENT_OPERATIONS.md.

[Unit]
Description=Rebuild coord webapp dist from merged main every 10 minutes (#1543, #2122)

[Timer]
OnBootSec=2min
OnUnitActiveSec=10min
AccuracySec=30s
Persistent=false

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
